# Docker build context for src/FoundryGate.Api/Dockerfile (built from the repo root).
# Keep the context small: only the projects the API references are COPYed anyway.
#
# The image COPYs exactly four projects — Domain, Data, Core, Api — plus the four root files
# every build needs (global.json, Directory.Build.props, Directory.Packages.props,
# NuGet.config) and .editorconfig, because warnings are errors and the analyzer config comes
# from it. Everything else in the repo is excluded below; anything added here that the
# Dockerfile does COPY fails the build loudly rather than silently, so this list is safe to
# keep tight (#157).
.git
.github
.claude
**/bin/
**/obj/
**/TestResults/
**/publish/
artifacts/
docs-site/
content/
infra/
plans/
scripts/
*.md
*.png
# Projects the API image never COPYs — the UI, the Functions host, the CLI, the SQL project
# and both test projects. Excluding them is what actually shrinks the context (the Blazor
# project alone drags in a wwwroot).
src/FoundryGate.Web/
src/FoundryGate.Functions/
src/FoundryGate.Cli/
src/FoundryGate.Database/
src/FoundryGate.Tests.Predeployment/
src/FoundryGate.Tests.Postdeployment/
FoundryGate.sln
GitVersion.yml
docker-compose.yml
.azurite/
# Local developer configuration must never reach an image. dockerignore patterns are
# CASE-SENSITIVE, and the repo's real file is `src/FoundryGate.Api/appsettings.local.json`
# (lowercase l) — the `appsettings.Local.json` line below matched nothing, so the image
# shipped it, connection string and all. Both casings are listed for each name rather than
# renaming the file, because the next one somebody adds will pick a casing at random.
# `verify-no-local-config.sh` is the check that stops this regressing.
**/appsettings.Development.json
**/appsettings.development.json
**/appsettings.Local.json
**/appsettings.local.json
**/appsettings.Secrets.json
**/appsettings.secrets.json
**/local.settings.json
**/Local.settings.json
